":"application","question":"For $A = \\{1,\\,2,\\,3\\}$, which statement is true?","options":["$4\\in A$","$2\\in A$","$2\\notin A$","$\\pi\\in A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"dc88389caba579090c18f26a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_set_a31623f2_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which membership statement about $\\pi$ and $\\mathbb{N}$ is true?","options":["$\\pi\\in\\mathbb{N}$","$\\mathbb{N}\\in\\pi$","$\\pi\\notin\\mathbb{N}$","$\\pi = \\mathbb{N}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2ea85e5bfbe99cc757be8f0e"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_set_a31623f2_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What determines equality of sets?","options":["Having the same elements","Writing the elements in the same order","Having a first and last element","Having a derivative equal to zero"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bb6535b3ff470bec8aedde26"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_set_a31623f2_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement correctly contrasts sets with ordered collections?","options":["Sets always list a first element.","Sets are always finite.","Sets cannot contain mathematical objects.","Sets do not impose order on their elements."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"881a501a23580e358d8ea9d2"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_set_a31623f2_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which set is equal to $\\{1,\\,2,\\,3\\}$?","options":["$\\{1,\\,2\\}$","$\\{3,\\,2,\\,1\\}$","$\\{1,\\,2,\\,4\\}$","$\\{1,\\,3\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e9bd41e7cf6013d44f3f65f7"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_set_a31623f2_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $A = \\{1,\\,2,\\,3\\}$, which statement correctly uses non-membership notation?","options":["$3\\notin A$","$1\\notin A$","$2\\notin A$","$4\\notin A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedba