_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the notation $x\\notin A$ mean?","options":["$x$ is not an element of $A$.","$x$ is the first element of $A$.","$x$ is equal to $A$.","$x$ is an ordered index of $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ef4781fb9b527dc16d07dd3c"},{"id":"set_new_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which set is described by the notation $\\{x\\in\\mathbb{R}:x>0\\}$?","options":["The set of all positive real numbers","the single number $0$","an ordered list of reals","all non-positive real numbers"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"06e4ea41d8cf22ea3c08687f"},{"id":"set_new_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Can a set be an element of another set?","options":["Yes; for example $\\{1\\}\\in\\{\\{1\\},2\\}$","No; elements must be numbers","No; sets only contain points","Only the empty set can be an element"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"20971d13d58fd2a008267fa5"},{"id":"set_new_017","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $\\{1,2\\}$, which is correct: $1\\in\\{1,2\\}$ or $1\\subseteq\\{1,2\\}$?","options":["$1\\in\\{1,2\\}$; $1$ is an element, not a subset","$1\\subseteq\\{1,2\\}$","both hold","Neither the element nor subset relation holds for this set."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"29077aebcc2215a0322adc7c"},{"id":"set_new_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $B=\\{a,b,c\\}$, which statement uses non-membership correctly?","options":["$a\\notin B$","$b\\notin B$","$c\\notin B$","$d\\notin B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"052b0458bef68ea1e0bd56e5"},{"id":"set_new_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why do $\\{1,2\\}$ and $\\{2,1\\}$ represent the same set?","options":["They have the same elements, and order is not part of set membership.","They have the same first element.","Every two finite sets are equal.","The symbol